<section class="section-tight" style="padding-top:0">
  <div class="wrap bi-grid">
    <article class="article">
      <div class="article-img"><img src="../assets/img/blog/alan-adi-ve-hosting-secimi.svg" alt=""></div>
      <p class="bi-lead">A website’s address and home are the two items most companies think about least, yet they hurt the most when something goes wrong. The wrong <b>domain and hosting choice</b> can lead to slow pages, emails that never arrive, or even the address you have used for years ending up in someone else’s hands.</p>
      <p>In this guide we explain the difference between a domain and hosting, how to choose a good domain, how .com.tr domains are obtained through TRABİS, why you should register your domain in your own name, and how to decide between options such as shared hosting, VPS and cloud.</p>
      <p>When we start corporate website projects, one of the first questions we ask our clients is “whose name is your domain registered in?” Seeing how often the answer is “we don’t know” convinced us this article was needed.</p>
      <h2 id="fark" class="bi-h2" data-n="01">The difference between a domain and hosting</h2>
      <p>Put simply, the domain is your site’s <b>address</b> and hosting is the <b>building</b> where your site’s files live. When a visitor types yourcompany.com.tr into the browser, DNS records tell it which server that address points to, and the site loads from that server.</p>
      <ul class="bi-list">
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Domain:</b> Rented annually and obtained through a registrar. Besides your site, it is also the basis of your corporate email addresses.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Hosting:</b> The server service on which your site’s files, database and often your email run. Paid monthly or annually.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>DNS:</b> The set of records that connects the domain to your hosting, email server and other services. It is entirely possible to buy your domain and hosting from different companies.</span></li>
      </ul>
      <p>Having your domain and hosting with the same company is convenient, but not required. In some cases keeping them separate even makes things easier when you want to switch hosting providers.</p>
      <h2 id="secim" class="bi-h2" data-n="02">How do you choose a good domain?</h2>
      <p>Your domain will appear for years on your business cards, vehicles, invoices and email signature. So keep these principles in mind:</p>
      <ul class="bi-list">
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Keep it short and easy to say:</b> Choose a name you don’t have to spell out letter by letter on the phone.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Avoid Turkish characters:</b> Domains containing ç, ğ, ı, ö, ş, ü are technically possible but cause problems in email and sharing. Watch out especially for confusion between “ı” and “i”.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Avoid hyphens and numbers:</b> They get lost when said aloud and are hard to remember.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Base it on your brand name:</b> Keyword-stuffed domains (e.g. cheapest-furniture-istanbul) no longer give an SEO advantage, and they don’t inspire trust either.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Consider close variations too:</b> Registering both the .com.tr and .com extensions protects your brand against imitations and typos.</span></li>
      </ul>
      <div class="bi-note"><svg class="ic" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M13 2L4 14h7l-1 8 9-12h-7l1-8z"/></svg><div><b>Trademark check:</b> Before buying a domain, use the Turkish Patent and Trademark Office’s search tool to see whether a similar registered trademark exists. A domain containing someone else’s trademark may become the subject of a dispute later.</div></div>
      <h2 id="comtr" class="bi-h2" data-n="03">.com or .com.tr? Getting a .tr domain through TRABİS</h2>
      <p>Domains with the “.tr” extension are managed through <b>TRABİS</b> (the .tr Network Information System), operated by the Information and Communication Technologies Authority (BTK). The most important change since TRABİS went live on 14 September 2022 is this: extensions such as <b>.com.tr, .net.tr and .org.tr</b> are now allocated without documentation on a “first come, first served” basis. .com.tr, which once required a trademark certificate or trade registry record, can now be obtained as easily as .com.</p>
      <p>Applications are made not directly to TRABİS but through BTK-accredited <b>registrars</b>. The current list of registrars is published at trabis.gov.tr. Documentation is still required for extensions reserved for certain institutions and professions, such as .av.tr, .edu.tr, .gov.tr and .bel.tr. According to publicly available data, the number of active .tr domains reached around 1.3 million by the end of 2025.</p>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Extension</th><th>Who is it for?</th><th>Note</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>.com.tr</td><td>Companies targeting the Turkish market</td><td>Strong local trust; can be obtained without documents</td></tr>
          <tr><td>.com</td><td>Brands selling internationally or targeting markets abroad</td><td>Most short, easy names are long gone</td></tr>
          <tr><td>.tr</td><td>Those who want a short, memorable address</td><td>Direct .tr allocation is also possible; ask your registrar about the rules</td></tr>
          <tr><td>.net, .org, new extensions</td><td>Special-purpose projects, campaigns</td><td>Usually a second choice as the main corporate address</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>For search engines, .com.tr naturally signals that you are targeting Türkiye. If you use .com, you send that signal through language, content and Search Console settings. In short, neither option is a problem for SEO; the real question is which market your brand speaks to.</p>
      <h2 id="mulkiyet" class="bi-h2" data-n="04">Domain ownership and renewal: the most common mistake</h2>
      <p>The legal owner of a domain is the person or company entered as the “registrant” at registration. Domains bought under an agency’s or an employee’s personal account cause serious trouble when the relationship sours or that person leaves the company.</p>
      <ul class="bi-list">
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Make your company the registrant:</b> Even if your agency handles the process, the domain should be registered under your company’s name.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Keep panel access yourself too:</b> The password and contact email of the registrar account should be tied to a permanent company address.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Turn on auto-renewal and extend the term:</b> Renewing the domain for several years at a time reduces the risk of forgetting.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Don’t miss reminder emails:</b> By regulation, the registrar must warn the domain holder before expiry; but that warning is useless if it goes to an email address nobody uses anymore.</span></li>
      </ul>
      <div class="bi-note"><svg class="ic" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M12 3l8 3v6c0 4.5-3.4 8.3-8 9-4.6-.7-8-4.5-8-9V6l8-3z"/><path d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"/></svg><div><b>Warning:</b> An expired domain is first suspended, then released for re-registration after a set waiting period. During this time your site and email stop working. A well-known brand address becoming available is an opportunity for others to grab it.</div></div>
      <h2 id="hosting" class="bi-h2" data-n="05">Hosting types: shared, VPS, cloud and managed servers</h2>
      <p>Hosting prices range from a few hundred lira to thousands; the difference usually lies in allocated resources, performance and who is responsible for what.</p>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Best suited for</th><th>Pros</th><th>Limitations</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Shared hosting</td><td>Low-traffic brochure sites</td><td>Cheap, easy to set up, control panel included</td><td>Resources are shared with other sites; if a neighbouring site slows down, so do you</td></tr>
          <tr><td>VPS / virtual server</td><td>Medium-traffic sites, e-commerce, custom software</td><td>Dedicated resources, full control</td><td>Updates and security management require technical know-how</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Cloud server</td><td>Growing projects with fluctuating traffic</td><td>Scale resources up and down as needed, high availability</td><td>Costs can climb if not planned properly</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Managed hosting</td><td>Businesses without a technical team</td><td>Maintenance, backups and security handled by the provider</td><td>Higher monthly fee</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>For a corporate brochure site, good-quality shared or managed hosting is usually enough. Systems with user logins, payments or heavy database work, however, need a VPS or cloud server. For example, in a residential complex management software like <a href="projelerimiz">KATSİS</a> the load rises sharply at the start of each month when residents pay their dues; for systems like this, infrastructure that can scale with demand is decisive.</p>
      <h2 id="kriter" class="bi-h2" data-n="06">8 criteria to check when choosing hosting</h2>
      <p>Instead of marketing phrases like “unlimited disk, unlimited traffic”, look at these concrete criteria:</p>
      <ul class="bi-list">
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>1. Performance:</b> SSD/NVMe disks, sufficient CPU and memory, current PHP or Node.js versions. Server response time directly determines your site’s speed (see <a href="site-hizi-core-web-vitals">site speed and Core Web Vitals</a>).</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>2. Server location:</b> If your visitors are in Türkiye, a data centre close to Türkiye or a CDN reduces latency.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>3. Personal data:</b> If the server is abroad, under KVKK this may mean personal data is being transferred abroad; address this in your privacy notice and contracts.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>4. Automatic backups:</b> Are daily backups taken, how many days are they kept, and is restoring them charged?</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>5. Free SSL:</b> Support for auto-renewing certificates such as Let’s Encrypt is a basic expectation today.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>6. Uptime commitment:</b> An availability commitment such as 99.9 percent, and what happens in case of an outage, should be written into the contract.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>7. Support:</b> A 24/7 support team in your language with real technical knowledge makes a difference when problems hit at night.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>8. Portability:</b> You should be able to download your files and database in full at any time and move to another provider.</span></li>
      </ul>
      <p>On the security side, keeping the server up to date, a firewall and malware scanning also matter. We cover these topics in detail in our article on <a href="web-sitesi-guvenligi">website security and SSL</a>.</p>
      <h2 id="tasima" class="bi-h2" data-n="07">Switching hosting or migrating your site: a seamless move</h2>
      <p>If your current hosting is slow, support is unresponsive or your site has grown, you may need to move. A well-planned migration goes so smoothly that visitors won’t notice:</p>
      <ul class="bi-list">
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Step 1 —</b> Take a full backup of the current site’s files and database; list your email accounts too.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Step 2 —</b> Set up the site on the new server and test it via a temporary address before changing DNS.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Step 3 —</b> Shorten the TTL of your DNS records a day before the move so the change propagates faster.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Step 4 —</b> Point DNS to the new server, and check that the SSL certificate has been issued and that forms and email work.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Step 5 —</b> Keep the old hosting active for a few more days; don’t shut it down until you are sure all traffic has moved to the new server.</span></li>
      </ul>
      <p>If you are also changing your URL structure, don’t forget to set up permanent (301) redirects from old URLs to new ones. Otherwise, search rankings built up over years can disappear quickly.</p>
      <h2 id="eposta" class="bi-h2" data-n="08">Don’t forget corporate email and DNS settings</h2>
      <p>One of the most valuable services that comes with your domain is corporate email in the form name@yourcompany.com.tr. Decide whether to get email within your hosting package or from a separate corporate email service based on your number of users and storage needs.</p>
      <p>Whichever route you choose, <b>SPF, DKIM and DMARC</b> records must be set up correctly in DNS so your emails don’t land in spam. Major email providers now require these authentication records from domains that send in bulk. If they are missing, even your most important proposal email may never reach the customer’s inbox.</p>
      <blockquote><div class="bi-q"><svg class="ic" width="22" height="22" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M9 7H5a1 1 0 0 0-1 1v4a1 1 0 0 0 1 1h3v1a3 3 0 0 1-3 3M19 7h-4a1 1 0 0 0-1 1v4a1 1 0 0 0 1 1h3v1a3 3 0 0 1-3 3"/></svg></div>Your domain is your digital title deed; you wouldn’t leave the deed in someone else’s name.</blockquote>
      <div class="bi-check">
        <div class="eyebrow">// DOMAIN AND HOSTING CHECKLIST</div>
        <ul class="bi-list"><li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span>Is the domain registered under your company’s name?</span></li><li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span>Can your company access the registrar panel?</span></li><li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span>Is auto-renewal on and the contact email up to date?</span></li><li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span>Does the hosting include daily backups and free SSL?</span></li><li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span>Have server location and KVKK implications been assessed?</span></li><li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span>Are SPF, DKIM and DMARC records configured?</span></li><li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span>Can you download your files and database in full?</span></li></ul>
      </div>
      <h2 id="sss" class="bi-h2" data-n="09">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="bi-faq"><details class="bi-faq-i"><summary>Do I need documents to get a .com.tr domain?</summary><p>No. Since TRABİS went live in 2022, .com.tr, .net.tr and .org.tr domains have been allocated to the first applicant without documentation. Documents are still required for extensions reserved for certain institutions, such as .av.tr, .edu.tr or .gov.tr.</p></details><details class="bi-faq-i"><summary>Should I buy my domain and hosting from the same company?</summary><p>It isn’t required. Buying from one company simplifies management; using separate companies means you don’t put your domain at risk when changing hosts. What matters is that the domain is registered in your name and the panel is under your control.</p></details><details class="bi-faq-i"><summary>What happens if my domain expires?</summary><p>An expired domain is first suspended, and the site and email stop working. If it isn’t renewed within a set grace period, it is deleted and released for re-registration; at that point someone else can take it. Auto-renewal and an up-to-date contact email eliminate this risk.</p></details><details class="bi-faq-i"><summary>What hosting is enough for a corporate site?</summary><p>For a corporate brochure site with a few thousand visits a month, good-quality shared or managed hosting is usually enough. For projects involving e-commerce, memberships or custom software, a VPS or cloud server is the better choice.</p></details><details class="bi-faq-i"><summary>Is .com or .com.tr better for SEO?</summary><p>Both are fine for SEO. .com.tr automatically signals that you are targeting Türkiye; with .com you can send the same signal through language, content and Search Console settings. Choose based on your target market and how memorable your brand is.</p></details></div>
      <h2 id="sonuc">Final word</h2>
      <p>The right domain and hosting choice means a short address that carries your brand, ownership registered to your company, auto-renewal, and a fast, backed-up server that fits your needs. Get these decisions right once and you won’t have to think about them for years.</p>
      <p>In our website projects we also handle domain and hosting setup, DNS and email configuration, with all rights remaining yours. If you are considering a site from scratch, you can first <a href="kurumsal-web-sitesi-rehberi">read our corporate website guide</a> or, to review your current infrastructure together, <a href="iletisim">get in touch with us</a>.</p>
